WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:06.340
I'm Amelia McNamara.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:10.110
I teach at the University of
Saint Thomas in Minnesota and I

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.330
tweet at AmeliaMN.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.490
I just tweeted a link
to these slides in case

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:17.120
you want to follow along.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.790
So I'm really
interested in R syntax

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:22.190
and also teaching statistics.

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:24.110
If you are someone who
teaches statistics,

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.840
that we are always
trying to have students

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.430
working with multivariate data.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:31.790
So in this example,
looking at the relationship

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:34.760
between Flipper length and
Bill length of penguins

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.730
between different species to
see if that relationship is

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:39.170
the same.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.750
And I chose this sort
of contrived example

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.300
to show you why educators
believe that we should not

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.980
be teaching base R syntax.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.460
And that is because it can
get really complicated, really

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:49.980
verbose.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.313
It's easy to make mistakes.

00:00:51.313 --> 00:00:52.730
And if you look
at those graphics,

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:55.500
they don't even have the
same consistent axes.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.830
So most educators
agree we should either

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.920
use the formula syntax like
from the mosaic package

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.000
or the tidyverse syntax.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.110
And both of those are going
to create beautiful plot two

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.870
graphics with those
consistent axes,

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:11.930
but we have lots of opinions
about which of these

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:15.410
we should use and
not a lot of data.

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:18.320
So I did a semester
long, head to head

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:22.310
comparison of the formula
and tidyverse syntax.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:24.270
Why did I do this to myself?

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:25.980
I basically doubled
my work, right?

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.940
I had to create the
materials in both syntax.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.640
I tried to make them as
consistent as possible in R

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.810
Markdown documents
that had the same text.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:36.680
The only thing that was
different was the R syntax.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:38.810
And the reason for
this is because I

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:40.560
wanted to get us some data.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.542
And I also really believe that
constraints breed creativity.

00:01:44.542 --> 00:01:46.250
If you put those
constraints on yourself,

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:48.710
it's going to make
you more creative.

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:50.130
So here are my results.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.490
Some things are
really easy regardless

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:56.250
of syntax. R Markdown
or quarto works so well.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.160
Inference works
well in intro stuff,

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.410
whether using the mosaic
package for inference

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:03.080
or the infer package
and the tidyverse.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:04.650
But different
things can be hard.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.770
So in the formula
syntax, dealing with

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:09.110
and explaining how we
deal with missing data,

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:10.410
that's pretty challenging.

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:11.310
There's options.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:14.600
There's no equal through
there use equal complete obvs.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.980
That was tough in the tidyverse.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.340
Dealing with and explaining
the relationship between two

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:21.450
categorical variables
was a challenge.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:24.770
So we're used to seeing two
categorical variables explained

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:25.860
in a two way table.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.560
That's not a tidy
data structure,

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.540
and it's harder for
me and for students

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.160
to conceptualize
of the relationship

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:32.612
when it's in that tidy format.

00:02:32.612 --> 00:02:34.070
And that can have
implications when

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:38.872
you do things like chi-square
tests and proportion tests.

00:02:38.872 --> 00:02:40.830
The other thing that I
was really interested in

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:44.200
is how much code you really
need in an intro stat course.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:45.930
And it turns out not that much.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:48.390
So in the formula section
I exposed students

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.020
to 37 functions.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.722
In the tidyverse section
they saw 50 functions.

00:02:52.722 --> 00:02:54.930
Those are a little bit
different in terms of numbers,

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:57.300
but both of them over
the course of 15 weeks,

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.540
totally reasonable.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:00.630
A big criticism of the
tidyverse in teaching

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.795
is, oh, there's so many
tidyverse functions.

00:03:02.795 --> 00:03:04.420
How would students
ever learn them all?

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.003
Well, they're not
going to learn them.

00:03:06.003 --> 00:03:08.383
You're just going to show
them the 50 that they need.

00:03:08.383 --> 00:03:10.800
And if you look at the top
five in each of these sections,

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.883
I think you'll kind of see
some of the differences

00:03:12.883 --> 00:03:13.690
here as well.

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:16.680
So in the tidyverse section,
I use the summarize function

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:17.260
a lot.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.500
Every time we did
summary statistics,

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.370
I use the plot function a lot.

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:24.360
Every time I built a graphic
versus in the formula section

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:27.300
in the top five, I have the
histogram that's apparently

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.100
the graphic that I made the most
and I have the mean function.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:32.150
So a specific summary
statistic and you'd

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:33.900
see some of those
things kind of coming up

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.880
in the tidyverse section if
I went further down the list.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:39.560
The other thing I
found is there was

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:41.540
a difference in the
amount of time students

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:44.320
were spending computing
on rstudio cloud, students

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:46.820
in the tidyverse section, it
looks like they spent more time

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:47.670
computing.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:48.850
Why is this?

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:50.010
I have no idea.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:51.950
I probably should have
collected some data,

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:52.980
done some interviews.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.870
Asked them, is it
because you're on there?

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:55.860
You're like, oh, it's so fun.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:56.670
I want to keep looking.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:57.510
I want to play around.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:58.940
Or is it because
it's really tough

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:00.470
and you're banging your
head against the wall

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:01.520
and you're frustrated?

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:02.310
I don't know.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:06.790
And that's a great avenue
for future investigations.

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:09.078
If you want to try something
like this yourself,

00:04:09.078 --> 00:04:10.870
if you want to know
how many R functions,

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:15.760
do I show my students, use the
R function, get parse data.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:19.149
And if you want to know how much
time each individual student is

00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:20.980
spending on rstudio
cloud, you can

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:23.530
dig into your browser's
developer tools

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:26.290
to sneak that data out.

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:29.230
If you want to know more about how I
did this and what I found out,

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:31.630
I've got a preprint
on the arXiv

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:34.780
and if you want my
materials like my

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:35.900
R Markdownn documents,

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:38.950
you want to see how I got
that data for all my functions

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:41.000
and how I analyze the
data from my paper?

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.170
You want to read the paper?

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:44.180
All of that is available.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:45.890
I'm happy to take
questions on Discord.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:48.000
And Thanks so much.

